  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nTuesday night through Thursday, an upper-level longwave trough will \nbecome established across the Northern Plains and Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley with multiple shortwave troughs/perturbations at its \nbase nudging a cold front southward into the CWA Wednesday. A few \nCAMs suggest that showers and thunderstorms will be ongoing across \nIA into northern MO Wednesday morning at the nose of a diminishing \nLLJ, but additional or renewed development is forecast during the \nafternoon and evening near the cold front across northeastern MO and \nwest-central IL. With 25 to 35 kt of deep-layer wind shear near the \nfront, there is the potential for a few strong to severe \nthunderstorms, but there is some uncertainty in the amount of \ninstability due to variations in BL moisture and the impact of the \nearly day precipitation/clouds. For context, the interquartile range \nof global ensemble model SBCAPE approximately spans 500 to 2000 \nJ/kg. Regardless, some upscale growth/increasing coverage of \nthunderstorms is expected Wednesday evening into overnight \nconsidering a large parallel front-shear component, with a \nseasonably strong nocturnal LLJ providing maintenance overnight. \nWith deep-warm cloud depths and anomalous precipitable water (80 to \n90th climatological percentile), there is at least a low threat of \nlocally heavy rainfall Wednesday night into Thursday morning with \nfront being slow-moving and conditional interface of the LLJ with \nthe southern flank of an MCS. However, the confidence needed to \nmessage severe weather or flash flooding at this time range is not \nyet high enough.\n\nOn Thursday, the cold front will stall somewhere across the CWA with \nmoisture further increasing/pooling along it, providing at least \nisolated redevelopment of showers and thunderstorms during the \nafternoon or evening, perhaps increasing in coverage overnight as \nanother, albeit weaker, LLJ develops. After Thursday, model \nguidance is in agreement that the upper-level longwave trough \nwill become more retracted to Canada and the far-northern CONUS.\nAs more active mid-level flow lifts northward and a potential \nweak cutoff low migrates eastward of the Mid- Mississippi River\nValley, ensemble model probabilities of showers and \nthunderstorms decrease each day through the weekend. Less and \nless precipitation/clouds and increasing low-level southerly \nflow will permit an eventual warming trend back to above \naverage, but it is uncertain how quickly this drying and \nwarming takes place, reflected by a 5 to 8 F interquartile \ntemperature spread in the NBM through the weekend.",
